      "text": "Let's be accurate with our calculations. First, let's denote the number of coins in the box as \\( N \\). According to the problem, when the coins are equally divided among six people, four coins are left over. This can be expressed as:\n\\[ N \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6} \\]\n\nSecondly, when the coins are equally divided among five people, three coins are left over. This can be expressed as:\n\\[ N \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\n\nWe need to find the smallest positive \\( N \\) that satisfies both congruences. We can start by writing \\( N \\) in terms of the first congruence:\n\\[ N = 6k + 4 \\]\nwhere \\( k \\) is an integer. Now, we substitute this expression into the second congruence:\n\\[ 6k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSimplifying this, we get:\n\\[ 6k + 4 \\equiv k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5} \\]\nor simply:\n\\[ k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5} \\]\nSince \\(-1\\) is congruent to \\(4\\) modulo 5, we can write:\n\\[ k = 5m + 4 \\]\nfor some integer \\( m \\). Now, substitute this back into the expression for \\( N \\):\n\\[ N = 6k + 4 = 6(5m + 4) + 4 = 30m + 24 + 4 = 30m + 28 \\]\nSo, \\( N \\) can be written as:\n\\[ N = 30m + 28 \\]\nThe smallest positive \\( N \\) occurs when \\( m = 0 \\), so:\n\\[ N = 28 \\]\n\nNow, we need to find how many coins are left when 28 coins are equally divided among seven people. We calculate:\n\\[ 28 \\div 7 = 4 \\]\nSo, when 28 coins are divided among seven people, each person gets 4 coins, and there are no coins left over. Therefore, the final answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{0} \\]",
