      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "1. We are given two conditions: when the coins are divided among six people, there are four coins left over, and when the coins are divided among five people, there are three coins left over. Thus, the box contains a number \\( N \\) that satisfies:\n   \\[\n   N \\equiv 4 \\pmod{6}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   N \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n\n2. We aim to find the smallest \\( N \\) that meets these congruences. \n\n3. First, express \\( N \\) in terms of the first condition:\n   \\[\n   N = 6k + 4, \\text{ for some integer } k\n   \\]\n\n4. Substitute this expression into the second condition:\n   \\[\n   6k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n\n5. Simplify the congruence:\n   \\[\n   6k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n   Reduce 6 modulo 5:\n   \\[\n   6 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n   Thus,\n   \\[\n   1k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   k + 4 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n\n6. Solve for \\( k \\) by isolating \\( k \\):\n   \\[\n   k \\equiv 3 - 4 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   k \\equiv -1 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n   Since equivalence can be negative,\n   \\[\n   k \\equiv 4 \\pmod{5}\n   \\]\n\n7. Substitute \\( k = 5m + 4 \\) back into the expression for \\( N \\):\n   \\[\n   N = 6k + 4\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   N = 6(5m + 4) + 4\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   N = 30m + 24 + 4\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   N = 30m + 28\n   \\]\n\n   Hence, the smallest \\( N \\) that meets the conditions occurs when \\( m = 0 \\):\n   \\[\n   N = 28\n   \\]\n\n8. Now consider \\( N = 28 \\) to be divided among seven people:\n   \\[\n   N \\div 7 = 28 \\div 7 = 4\n   \\]\n\n   The Remainder when 28 is divided by 7 is 0, meaning all coins are evenly divisible.\n\n   \\(\\boxed{0}\\)",
